(slope -1/3). For q=4 => p=-2 (slope -1/2). Wait p = -2, q=4 => slope -0.5, but p+q = 2? -2+4=2, yes. So slope -1/2 also appears with q=4, p=-2. But slope -1/2 is already covered. For q=5 => p=-3 (slope -3/5). So there are many possibilities with p+q=2, but the bound may be less than floor((k+1)/2) for those? Let's examine the bound: For p+q = 2, |p+q| = 2, so bound (2.4) gives floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). So any line with |p+q| = 2 can achieve the bound. However, does any such line actually intersect T_k in exactly floor((k+1)/2) points? The bound is derived from the sum x+y changes by p+q = 2 per step. So the number of integer points in T_k is limited by the range of sum values. If the line's sum values start at some minimal sum s0 and increase by 2 each step, the number of points is roughly floor((max_sum - min_sum)/2)+1. To achieve the maximum possible number of points, we need the line to have points covering the entire range of sums from the minimal possible sum (≥2) to the maximal possible sum (≤k+1). Since the step is 2, we can cover at most floor((k+1-2)/2)+1 = floor((k-1)/2)+1 = floor((k+1)/2). So any line with p+q = 2 can achieve this bound if its sum values align appropriately. But not all such lines will have integer points for each sum value. For a line to have integer points for each sum value in the range, we need the line to intersect each diagonal x+y = s for s = s0, s0+2, s0+4,... up to ≤ k+1. The line's integer points are given by (x0+qt, y0+pt). The sum x+y = (x0+y0)+(p+q)t = s0 + 2t. So each integer t yields a sum that increments by 2. So the line will have integer points for each sum value in that arithmetic progression. So the number of points is exactly the number of t values such that the point lies within the triangle (i.e., x>0, y>0, x+y ≤ k+1). So the bound is achieved if the line's points fill the entire possible range of sums from the minimal sum (≥2) to the maximal sum (≤k+1) with step 2. That requires that the line's points start at sum s0 = 2 (or maybe 3) and go up to k+1 (or k). Let's examine the three lines given: y = x has points (1,1) sum=2, (2,2) sum=4, (3,3) sum=6,... So sums are even numbers from 2 up to k+1 if k+1 is even (i.e., k odd). For k odd, k+1 is even, so the line y=x covers sums 2,4,...,k+1. That's floor((k+1)/2) points. Good.\n\nLine y = -2x + (2m+3) for k=2m+1: points (1,2m+1) sum=2m+2 = k+1, (2,2m-1) sum=2m+1 = k, (3,2m-3) sum=2m = k-1, etc. So sums decreasing by 2 from k+1 down to 2? Actually last point (m+1,1) sum=m+2? Wait for m=1 (k=3), points (1,3) sum=4, (2,1) sum=3. So sums are 4,3? That's not step 2. Let's compute general: For line y = -2x + (2m+3), sum = x + (-2x + (2m+3)) = -x + (2m+3). So sum decreases by 1 per increase in x. Wait p+q = -2 + 1 = -1? Actually p = -2, q = 1 => p+q = -1. But they said |p+q| = 2? Wait p+q = -2 + 1 = -1, absolute value 1. But earlier they said lines with |p+q| = 2 are the only ones achieving bound. But this line has |p+q| = 1. However, they argued that for |p+q| = 1, the bound is also floor((k+1)/2) due to q≥2? Wait for p = -2, q = 1, q=1, but they said q≥2 for |p+q| = 1 case. Actually they argued that if |p+q| = 1, then q≥2 because otherwise p=0 (horizontal) or p = -q (slope -1). But here p = -2, q = 1 => p+q = -1, q=1, p ≠ 0, p ≠ -q (since -2 ≠ -1). So q=1 is allowed? But they said q≥2 for |p+q| = 1. Let's examine: p+q = 1 => p = 1 - q. If q = 1, then p = 0 => horizontal line (forbidden). So p+q = 1 forces q≥2. Similarly, p+q = -1 => p = -1 - q. If q = 1, then p = -2, which is allowed (p ≠ 0, p ≠ -q). So p+q = -1 with q=1 is allowed. So the line y = -2x + c corresponds to p = -2, q = 1, p+q = -1. So |p+q| = 1. So the bound for |p+q| = 1 case is not automatically floor((k+1)/2) but they derived a bound using q≥2 for p+q = 1 case, but for p+q = -1 case, q can be 1. So the bound may be larger? Let's examine the bound for |p+q| = 1 case: They derived that the number of admissible t is at most floor(k/q)+1 ≤ floor((k+1)/2). This us