over these with 2 sunny lines. Can we? Let's see if we can cover (2,1) and (2,2) with a sunny line: line through (2,1) and (2,2) is vertical (non-sunny). So cannot. (2,1) and (3,1) is horizontal (non-sunny). (2,2) and (3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-2) = -1, not allowed. So any line covering two of these points is non-sunny. So we need three sunny lines to cover each point individually, but we have only two. So fails.\n\nCase 2: Non-sunny line is vertical x=2. Then points (2,1),(2,2) covered. Remaining: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(3,1). Need to cover with 2 sunny lines. Could we cover (1,1) and (3,1) with a sunny line? That's horizontal, not allowed. (1,1) and (1,2) vertical, not allowed. (1,1) and (1,3) vertical, not allowed. (1,2) and (1,3) vertical. (1,2) and (3,1) slope = (1-2)/(3-1) = -1/2, which is allowed (slope -0.5). That's not 0, infinite, -1. So a sunny line can cover (1,2) and (3,1). That line also passes through (2,1.5) which is not integer. So we can cover those two points with one sunny line. Then we have (1,1) and (1,3) left, which cannot be covered by a single sunny line (vertical). So need two more sunny lines, but we have only one left. So fails.\n\nCase 3: Non-sunny line is vertical x=3. Then points (3,1) covered. Remaining: (1,1),(1,2),(1,3),(2,1),(2,2). Need to cover with 2 sunny lines. Could we cover (1,1) and (2,2) with line slope 1 (allowed). That covers those two. Then left (1,2),(1,3),(2,1). Could we cover (1,2) and (2,1) with line slope -1 (not allowed). (1,2) and (1,3) vertical (not allowed). (1,2) and (2,1) slope -1, not allowed. (1,3) and (2,1) slope = (1-3)/(2-1) = -2, allowed (slope -2). So we could cover (1,3) and (2,1) with a sunny line. Then left (1,2) alone, need a third sunny line. So fails.\n\nCase 4: Non-sunny line is horizontal y=1. Then points (1,1),(2,1),(3,1) covered. Remaining: (1,2),(1,3),(2,2). Need to cover with 2 sunny lines. Could we cover (1,2) and (2,2) with horizontal line y=2 (non-sunny). So not allowed. (1,2) and (1,3) vertical (