2,2), (n-2,3). That's three points. If we have only two sunny lines, can we cover these three points using sunny lines? Sunny lines can have any slope except 0, ∞, -1. Could a sunny line pass through two of these points? Possibly. Let's see: The points (n-2,1) and (n-2,2) have same x coordinate, so any line through them is vertical (x=n-2), which is non-sunny. So a sunny line cannot pass through both (n-2,1) and (n-2,2). Similarly, (n-2,2) and (n-2,3) vertical. So any sunny line can pass through at most one of these three points (since any line through two of them would be vertical). However, a sunny line could pass through (n-2,1) and some other point not in same column, but that would cover only one of the three points. So to cover all three points, we need at least three lines that are either vertical (non-sunny) or sunny lines each covering one point. Since we have n-2 non-sunny lines, we could allocate a vertical line x=n-2 as one of them, covering all three points. That would be a non-sunny line. So we need at least one non-sunny line for column n-2. That's fine. So the existence of a vertical line for column n-2 is not a problem.\n\nBut the solution says \"If k≤2, we need a vertical line for this column.\" Indeed, we need a non-sunny line for column n-2. That's okay.\n\nNow we need to consider other columns: x=n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) (since b ≤ n+1 - (n-1) = 2). So two points. Similarly, x=n has point (n,1). So we have columns with 2 and 1 points.\n\nIf we have exactly two sunny lines, we need to allocate them to cover some points. The non-sunny lines can be vertical lines for columns, but we have only n-2 non-sunny lines. We can use vertical lines for columns x=1,...,x=n-2 (that's n-2 columns). That would cover all points except those in columns n-1 and n. But we have only n-2 non-sunny lines, so we can assign vertical lines to columns 1 through n-2. Then we have two sunny lines left to cover points in columns n-1 and n. Let's see if we can cover them with two sunny lines.\n\nPoints in column n-1: (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Points in column n: (n,1). So total three points. Could we cover them with two sunny lines? Possibly. For example, one sunny line could pass through (n-1,1) and (n,1) (horizontal line y=1). But horizontal lines are non-sunny (parallel to x-axis). So not allowed. So we cannot use horizontal line. Could we use a sunny line that passes through (n-1,1) and (n-1,2)? That's vertical, non-sunny. So not allowed. Could we use a sunny line that passes through (n-1,2) and (n,1)? That line has slope (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/1 = -1, which is slope -1, i.e., line x+y = constant, which is non-sunny. So not allowed. So any line through any two of these three points is either vertical, horizontal, or slope -1, all non-sunny. So a sunny line can cover at most one of these three points. So we need at least three sunny lines to cover them, unless we use non-sunny lines for some of them. But we have used all n-2 non-sunny lines for columns 1 through n-2. Could we instead use a non-sunny line for column n-1 (vertical line x=n-1) covering both points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2)? That would be a non-sunny line, but we have only n-2 non-sunny lines. If we use vertical lines for columns 1 through n-3 (that's n-3 lines) and also vertical line for column n-1 (makes n-2 non-sunny lines), then we have left column n-2 and column n uncovered. Column n-2 has three points; we could use a sunny line to cover some of them, but we need to cover all three. As we argued, a sunny line can cover at most one of those three points. So we would need at least three sunny lines for column n-2, plus maybe one for column n. That would be >2 sunny lines. So k=2 seems impossible.\n\nBut we need to be thorough: maybe we can use non-sunny lines of type x+y=const (slope -1) to cover some points. Let's examine possibilities.\n\nWe have three types of non-sunny lines: vertical (x=const), horizontal (y=const), and diagonal (x+y=const). The diagonal lines have s